    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2037239300295659794",
      "url": "https://x.com/JJensen2020/status/2037239300295659794",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/JJensen2020/status/2037239300295659794",
      "text": "Strait of Hormuz Updates: \n\nThere is still a complete lack of clarity on what the status is on negotiating a deal between the U.S. and Iran. The admin says one thing and shortly after Iranian official deny the claims.\n\nThis means that the Strait of Hormuz remains essentially closed at around 4% of the normal traffic, with potentially more ships turning off their transponders to avoid being targeted. \n\nIran has at times offered to allow some tankers and cargo ships to go through depending on if they are “non hostile”, aligned in some way, or pay a toll. This has been spotty and inconsistent at best. \n\nThere remains a global energy crisis that is slowly becoming a major concern, especially in the pacific region that is heavily reliant on Middle Eastern LNG and oil. \n\nChina, Japan, India, Vietnam, Thailand, Sri Lanka, South Korea, Australia, New Zealand, and the Philippines are taking or will soon take measures to address skyrocketing prices and shortages.\n\nAlso at risk is the world’s food production as a significant amount of fertilizer is now out of reach. In addition to around 30% of certain fertilizers taken offline due to Hormuz, China has banned 50%-75% of its own fertilizer exports which could amount to an additional 8% of the world’s supply. \n\nThis will have major knock on inflationary effects as the year goes on as well as contribute to food shortages in certain parts of the world as yields decline. \n\nIn short, the Strait of Hormuz needs to open up otherwise the global economy is in deep trouble. \n\n#Iran #China #StraitofHormuz #Trade #Trump #IranWar‌",
      "source": "Twitter for iPhone",
